Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Properties of Trotter-Suzuki manifolds", "Title",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.943697526954799*^9, 3.943697584470463*^9}, {
   3.943697641662852*^9, 3.94369765080263*^9}, 3.977475938103941*^9, 
   3.977487914045478*^9, {3.977487958194963*^9, 3.977487962966404*^9}, 
   3.977488034718924*^9, {3.999596078273815*^9, 3.999596213555319*^9}, {
   3.9995965893985233`*^9, 
   3.999596591207323*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f42094f7-5dca-d847-a535-\
1726b66fc62e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The notebook analyses the Jacobian generated from error coefficients \
defined by the Omelyan method for construction of high order Trotter-Suzuki \
schemes. For order n=6 this can still be done analytically, while at order \
n=8 the computation becomes very costly, and numerics need to be introduced. \
Once the Jacobian is known, certain properties like the singular values, rank \
and null space can give insight into the structure of the manifolds generated \
by the error function ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["Err", "n"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "8e239f3a-588b-5343-8c31-e3c1914b3296"],
 ". The analyses orders both include an non-generic manifold composed of \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]accidental\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] solutions, which \
are of interest to the understanding of the behaviour at high orders."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977487529804587*^9, 3.977487588153356*^9}, {
  3.999596349038378*^9, 3.999596563739216*^9}, {3.9995966013328667`*^9, 
  3.999596735383484*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a07be687-69f5-9d43-900d-\
6a60bdb62f0f"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Order 6", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.998638517460739*^9, 3.998638521561556*^9}, 
   3.998639974381716*^9, {3.9995962365671005`*^9, 3.999596243950224*^9}, {
   3.9995962784321404`*^9, 
   3.999596301867075*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"dbfb7590-1b4f-49bd-b315-\
efa23a2ce730"],

Cell["\<\
At order n=6 the error coefficients can be identified analytically, with \
relatively low cost. This allows the inspection of the whole manifold easily. \
By evaluating the Jacobian in the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]accidental\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] solutions at q = 7, 8 cycles, we find the \
over-constrained region, which should not have solutions. Incidentally, in \
these specific points the constraints are resolved and 3 solutions can be \
found despite the lack of parameters. Once we cross over to the \
under-constrained region at q=9 cycles, the true manifold of solutions \
returns, and the rank starts growing as normal.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977487529804587*^9, 3.977487588153356*^9}, {
  3.999596349038378*^9, 3.999596563739216*^9}, {3.9995966013328667`*^9, 
  3.9995970215021896`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"10a18685-3649-ff4d-a970-\
11e2819b729a"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["7 cycles", "Subsection",
